# The REPOSITORY's own ignores. `amik/.gitignore` is a different file
# with a different job: it travels with the folder into any project that
# copies it, so the runtime rules live there and not here.

.venv/
__pycache__/
*.egg-info/
build/
dist/
.pytest_cache/

# Working notes, not documentation. Parked seeds and an agent-landscape
# survey -- neither finished enough to publish nor useless enough to
# delete. Revisit when this repository has a docs story.
/SOMEDAY.md
/RESEARCH.md

# Amik's OWN board, and it is IGNORED -- the opposite of what this
# block said until 2026-09-19, when the repository acquired a remote it
# would be published to.
#
# This directory is two things at once: the tool, and one board being
# worked with it. As the tool it ships `amik/starter/`, which is the
# template and the only copy anyone needs. As a board it holds whoever
# is working here and what they are thinking about next, which is
# theirs and not the project's.
#
# So the board joins the rest of the runtime state `amik/.gitignore`
# already hides -- the lock, the seen cursor, the transcripts, the
# prototypes. It was the last piece still tracked, and it was tracked
# only to demonstrate Amik being built with Amik, which cannot coexist
# with a board that is private. The README carries that instead.
#
# A fresh clone has no board, which is correct: `amik serve` writes one
# from the starter on first run, the same way any other project gets
# one. A clone that HAD one could not, because seeding refuses an
# instance that already has a board.
#
# This is about THIS repository, which is why it is here and not in
# `amik/.gitignore`. A project that adopts Amik should track its board:
# a board that lives in your repository is the whole premise, and
# nothing copies this file into one.
amik/board.jsonl
amik/cards/

# The agent's raw output from a run, when [loop] transcript is on.
# Every file it read, which is exactly what should not be committed.
amik/runs/

# The board's own history, which travels with the board it describes.
amik/log.jsonl
